{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED at exact clean pushed head b9483eaad39d27bc2747f5e8ec0c1501d03e4e2f, against repair child 144042f6 and my ratified changes-requested verdict 09884dd8, co-signing all ten exact-path artifacts.\n\nTHE SUCCESSOR IS TEST-ONLY, VERIFIED BY DIFF RATHER THAN BY REPORT. git diff 5aab3697..b9483eaa is one file, cmd/chess/main_test.go, 44 insertions and 24 deletions. Filtering the changed-path list for anything that is not a test file returns nothing. No production file moved, which was the first condition and the one easiest to violate quietly.\n\nTHE BLOCKER IS CLOSED, AND I HAVE THE EVIDENCE I ASKED FOR RATHER THAN A REPORT THAT IT IS CLOSED. All four seat-copy mutations are now caught: the White seat line of game.html, the Black seat line of game.html, the White branch of lobby.html, and the Black branch of lobby.html. Each was applied alone to the clean head, run, and restored, with the tree confirmed empty and HEAD unchanged between every one.\n\nThe part that actually settles it is the negative control. The two White mutations were replayed against the OLD test file from 5aab3697 and both survive there, green on the whole package; under the new test file both die. That is a direct demonstration that the added black-created fixtures are load-bearing rather than decorative, and it reproduces the original rejection exactly before closing it. This is what I meant when I said a report that the mutation now fails is not a substitute for watching it fail, and it is the difference between a guard and the appearance of one.\n\nMutation A trips on the restricted table rather than the open one, which is the right place: reverting White to unconditional Open mislabels the white-empty restricted game while the white-empty open game still renders correctly. The guard fires on the branch the mutation actually broke, not on a neighbour.\n\nTHE OLD GUARDS SURVIVED RATHER THAN BEING DISPLACED. This was the second thing I was watching for, because widening a fixture is a common way to quietly lose coverage. The Black-seat assertions on both surfaces, the invite-restricted case, the private-material leak check and the board-JSON admission predicate are all still present with the same must-contain and must-not-contain polarity; the refactor drove them from a table with a per-case empty-side rather than deleting and replacing them. Two changes go the other way and improve things: t.Fatal became t.Errorf where the check now sits inside a loop, which is correct, and the new page helper adds an HTTP status check the open-game path previously did not have. The leak assertion now runs over three games instead of two.\n\nLIVE, ALL SIX COMBINATIONS. Six games were created — open, secret-restricted and invite-restricted, each with both creator colours — served, and read over HTTP. Every empty seat renders truthful wording on both the game page and the lobby card, the two surfaces agree in all six, and the open-seat MCP invitation appears only for the two genuinely open games, on whichever side is empty. That is the class of defect this lane has now closed in three separate places.\n\nARCHITECTURE: preserved, and there is still no architecture reference page in this repository — README.md is the only markdown file, so there is nothing to check against and nothing to update. I say that explicitly rather than by omission. The head touches no production layer at all; the domain, the read handler, the CLI and both templates are byte-identical to 5aab3697. The admission contract is unchanged; the test simply now observes both halves of it.\n\nSECURITY: no new exposure, and the security guard is strengthened rather than merely preserved. The leak assertion extends to a third game, so a white-empty restricted page is now covered. Verified independently against real served bytes across thirteen artifacts: zero hits on the plaintext secret, on both plausible digests of it, on the invited fingerprint, on the private field names, and on the join invitation URL that the CLI returns to its caller but must never serve. Restricted board JSON exposes admission_open and public fields only, with no key emitted for the empty seat.\n\nI want to record how that scan was validated, because it is the check most likely to be believed without examination. The first run reported every pattern clean including seven positive controls that must hit — an unquoted file list under zsh handed grep one nonexistent path. The controls caught it. The reported result is the corrected run, and its controls demonstrably fire: the game names, admission_open, the White seat markup and the page wordmark all hit with the counts you would expect. A leak scan whose controls do not fire is not evidence of anything, and this is the second time in this lane that same shell defect has produced a false clean.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION: the head is itself a simplification and I found nothing to cut. Collapsing three near-identical request blocks into one helper is why the diff adds only 44 lines while covering two more fixtures and five more assertions, and driving both seats from a single empty-side field keeps the polarity in one place instead of duplicating the White case. Asking for further cuts here would weaken the conditions.\n\nWHAT IS FRESH AND WHAT CARRIES FORWARD, so this verdict is not read as more than it is. Fresh this pass: the test-only diff, the four mutations and the two negative controls, the survival of the old guards, the six live combinations, the leak scan, and the gates at this exact head — go build and go vet clean, go test -count=1 ok on both packages, go test -race -count=1 ok on both, with the target test run three times for stability and the full suite twice. Carried forward from c603aa6b on ba0e06bc and NOT re-derived here: the orphan-game reproduction and its disappearance, the twelve mutations of that pass, and the cross-version fold refusal checked by building binaries at two heads and crossing them. Carried forward from 09884dd8: the admission predicate analysis and the fourth-surface search.\n\nTHREE NON-BLOCKING OBSERVATIONS, none of which should hold this head. The white-restricted fixture reuses the same secret file as the existing restricted game, which is harmless today because no idempotency key is passed and the names differ, but would become a collision if a future change folded the secret into a derived key — worth remembering given that this lane already fixed one derived-key bounds defect. The invite-restricted variant with an empty White seat is not asserted, which adds no template branch since both restriction kinds reach the same arm, and I confirmed it live regardless. And the failure messages dump the whole page, which buried the assertion line during the mutation runs; that is style carried forward from the previous head, not introduced here.\n\nNOT VERIFIED, stated plainly. No page was rendered in a browser, so layout, CSS and app.js runtime are unchecked. The join flow was not exercised against the new fixtures, so I confirmed that a white-empty restricted game labels itself truthfully but not that it admits the right actor — that authority behaviour lives in seat_authority_test.go and is untouched here. The leak scan covered the lobby, six game pages and six board responses, but not /v1/games, /v1/legal or the MCP tool surface. Mutation coverage was the four seat branches only; I make no claim about the suite's strength elsewhere. All gates ran on darwin/arm64 only.\n\nThe merge remains held for hugh's fresh-user acceptance of the parent request d7c81f7a, as codex proposed and I agreed. This approval clears the review gate and nothing beyond it.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:1b96687964cbba5a6089c526d7136b32ba9488a5#git:sha1:6b0da1ee21a08382499dbfa921aaa6760fa2c419","head":"b9483eaad39d27bc2747f5e8ec0c1501d03e4e2f","verdict":"approved"}}